The empty lesson prose supplies no definition of N, [x] or {x}. Below, the
floor-domain calculations are conditional on [x]=floor(x), {x}=x-floor(x).
This convention must be visible as an authored condition in every affected
question, not reported as a sourced lesson definition. The source itself's
notation is not resolved by this conditional calculation.

Figure 76 source pixels (opened once): vertical y axis and horizontal x axis;
half-unit grid, x labels -3,-2,-1,0,1; positive y labels 1 through 8;
three decreasing positive curves labelled y=0.25^x, y=(1/3)^x, y=0.8^x;
all pass through (0,1). Left of the y axis 0.25 is uppermost and 0.8
lowermost; right of it the order reverses. This is source interpretation,
not a repeat of the figure's existing PASS review. Source page 206 and
borrowed=true are caller-supplied provenance; do not infer other page facts.

682. Task: compare three exponential pairs. Since 6/7 < 7/8,
sqrt(3)<sqrt(7), and -sqrt(5)<-sqrt(3), all three first expressions
are smaller than their second expressions. Increasing base 2 preserves
the order even for negative irrational exponents.

683. Task: compare eight expressions with 1, including variable cases.
a<1, b>1, c<1, d>1, e>1 (2-sqrt(2)>0), f<1 (1-sqrt(3)<0).
For g, 2^x is below/equal/above 1 as x<0 / x=0 / x>0.
For h, 2^(1-x) is below/equal/above 1 as x>1 / x=1 / x<1.
Do not fix x to obtain a single comparison.

684. Task: image sets with endpoint topology.
a (1,infinity); b (0,1); c (2,32); d [1/2,2]; e [1,2].
Strict monotonicity transports finite endpoints, retains their open/closed
status, and an unattained horizontal asymptote does not become an endpoint.

685. Task: six inverse exponential equations. RHS are 2^5,2^-6,2^-1,
2^-2,2^-3,2^7; uniqueness gives x=5,-6,-1,-2,-3,7 respectively.

686. Task: four solution sets.
a [8,infinity); b (-infinity,-2]; c (-7,infinity); d (-infinity,1).
Rewrite 256=2^8, .25=2^-2, 1/128=2^-7, 2=2^1.

687. Task: compare two pairs of vertical changes from the graph.
For width h>0, delta at starting x=t is 2^t(2^h-1).
a later interval [1.5,2.5] has twice the change of [.5,1.5].
b later interval [1.2,1.7] has twice the change of [.2,.7].
Both are vertical changes, not final heights or slopes at isolated points.
An actual graph must remain available; algebra alone loses the requested route.

688. Task: sketch both curves together, solve equality and both inequalities
graphically. Positive increasing curves meet only at (0,1).
1.5^x/2^x=(.75)^x, so equality x=0, first smaller for x>0,
first larger for x<0. Curve order reverses across the common point.
Provide actual visible graphs; recognition cannot establish drawing ability.

689. Task: two triples of equality/greater/less solution sets.
Both ratios 15/8 and .9/.8 are >1. In both triples equality {0},
greater (0,infinity), less (-infinity,0). Comparing bases rather than
checking the exponent's sign gives the wrong conclusion for negative x.

690. Task: two mutual-position comparisons of the actual three curves.
For x>0: .25^x < (1/3)^x < .8^x.
For x<0: .25^x > (1/3)^x > .8^x. At zero all equal 1.
Use source/modern Figure 76, never substitute a symbolic-only prompt.

691. Task: sketch two arbitrary curves for 0<a<b<1.
Both decrease, are positive, pass through (0,1), approach zero on the right,
and grow without bound on the left. a^x<b^x for x>0 and a^x>b^x for x<0.
Their precise steepness is not fixed by the source; any numerical example
must be identified as a representative, not silently set a and b.

692. Task: sketch bases 1/2,1/3 and place .4 between them.
Since 1/3<.4<1/2, for x>0: (1/3)^x<.4^x<(1/2)^x;
for x<0 the order reverses. All pass through (0,1) and decrease.
The middle graph is not an arithmetic average of heights.

693. Task: for both base regimes, sketch a^x and the constant a, compare.
They meet at (1,a) only. If a>1, a^x<a for x<1 and >a for x>1.
If 0<a<1, a^x>a for x<1 and <a for x>1.
The threshold is 1, not 0, despite the common exponential reference (0,1).

694. Task: three comparisons using reciprocals.
a .04^-5=25^5 <25^6; b (2/3)^-6=(3/2)^6=1.5^6;
c .5^-11=2^11. A negative exponent reciprocates the base, not the value's sign.

695. Task: infer k,m order for four bases.
a k>m because pi>1.
b k<m because pi/3>1 (pi>3), despite the base being close to 1.
c k<m because 0<sqrt(3)-sqrt(2)<1 and order reverses.
d k<m because sqrt(7)-1>1.

696. Task: four comparisons to 1. a<1, b<1, c>1, d>1.
For c sqrt(15)/4<1 and exponent negative; for d sqrt(3)-sqrt(2)
is between 0 and 1 and exponent negative. Do not treat irrational bases as >1.

697. Task: infer the signs of four exponents.
a positive, b negative, c positive, d negative. Compare RHS with 1;
then use base monotonicity. The magnitude 1.003 being near 1 does not make k zero.

698. Task: graphically solve two intersection problems.
a roots 0 and 1, since 2^0=1 and 2^1=2. f=2^x-x-1 is strictly convex
and these two roots exhaust its zeros.
b unique root 0: (.3)^x is strictly decreasing and x+1 increasing;
the LHS positive also excludes x<=-1. Actual graphs must show both curves.

699. Task: graphically solve 2^x=x^2-2, without assuming two roots.
For x<0, f=2^x-x^2+2 has f'>0; f(-2)<0<f(-1), giving one negative
root, numerically -1.5316287576583387 by independently computed bisection.
For x>=0, f'=ln(2)2^x-2x, f''=(ln(2))^2 2^x-2 has exactly one zero.
Thus f' first decreases then increases, with one zero between 0 and 1
(local maximum) and one between 3.2 and 3.3 (local minimum).
At the latter, f>2^3.2-3.3^2+2>.29, and f(0)=3; hence no nonnegative roots.
The minimum occurs at x about 3.2124325245 and is positive. A graph should
show the near-miss on the positive side rather than crop it away.
Graph readout can locate a root in (-1.6,-1.5), not prove completeness on R.

700. Task: six root sets.
a 3^x=3^-2/3 gives {-2/3}.
b .4^x=(2/5)^x=25/4=(2/5)^-2 gives {-2}.
c 7^x=7^-1/5 gives {-1/5}.
d (.8*1.25)^10=1 gives {0}.
e .2^x=5^-x=5^3 gives {-3}.
f 6^(x^2-1)=1 implies x^2-1=0, roots {-1,1}.

701. Task: six inequalities.
a x>3/2; b x<2/3; c x>-1/2;
d .7^x>100/49=.7^-2 implies x<-2;
e .2^x<25=.2^-2 implies x>-2;
f (1/3)^x>(1/3)^(2/3) implies x<2/3.
All bounds are strict; bases below 1 reverse order.

702. Task: four exponent-linear equations.
a 5x=-7 => -7/5.
b (-2x)=-3 for base .5 => x=3/2 (equivalently 2^(2x)=2^-3?
Correction of the alternate conversion: .5^(-2x)=2^(2x), .125=2^-3,
so 2x=-3 and x=-3/2. The direct base-.5 exponent of .125 is +3,
so -2x=3. Final independent result b=-3/2.)
c 3x=0 =>0. d 7x=2 =>2/7.
The retained correction records the original independent arithmetic fault;
the final result is checked by substitution, not by an answer file.

703. Task: ten exponential equations.
a 2^(x/2)=2^10 =>20.
b 10^(x/2)*10^-1=10^3 =>8.
c 2^(6x-2)=2^(6x-15) would require -2=-15; no roots.
d 2^(4x-2)=2^(70-5x) =>x=8.
e 10^x=10^-3 =>-3.
f 6^x=6^3 =>3.
g 10^(x-1)=10^-3 =>-2.
h 28^(1-x)=28^-1 =>2.
i (sqrt(12)*sqrt(3))^x=6^x=6^-1 =>-1.
j (sqrt(14))^(x-1)=1 =>1.
The impossible c is an essential distinction, not a missing numeric blank.

704. Task: three inequalities.
a 3^(x/2+1)>3^-3 => x>-8.
b 3^(3x+1-x)=3^(2x+1)<3^-1 =>x<-1.
c 2^(-6x+1/3)>1 =>x<1/18.

705. Task: three equations.
a 3^(4x-4)=3^5 =>9/4.
b 6^(4x+6)=6^3 =>-3/4.
c 8^-1*2^3=1 =>(x-1)^2=0 =>{1}, one distinct root.

706. Task: four factorizable sums.
a (1+4)2^x=20 =>2^x=4 =>2.
b (1+3)3^x=12 =>3^x=3 =>1.
c (5+1)5^(x-1)=30 =>5^(x-1)=5 =>2.
d (1+4+16)4^(x-1)=84 =>4^(x-1)=4 =>2.
Addition of exponentials is factored, never handled as addition of exponents.

707. Task: prove both directions of a geometric/exponential correspondence.
The approved modern erratum ca -> ca^x is already in the seed. It does not
settle domains. For indexing n=1,2,... and r!=0, u_n=u_1 r^(n-1)
=c a^n with a=r,c=u_1/r, including negative r on this integer domain.
Conversely u_n=c a^n satisfies u_(n+1)=a u_n for integer n>=1.
For a!=0 and c=0 this is the zero sequence, valid under the recurrence
definition but adjacent quotients are undefined. For a=0 and n>=1 all
terms are zero; a source geometric sequence (u_1!=0,0,0,...) cannot be
represented by c*0^n with finite c. Thus an unrestricted 'any' in a is
false under that recurrence definition and starting index.
For n=0,1,..., u_n=u_0 r^n, but r=0 needs an explicit 0^0 convention
or a separate n=0 definition. Negative a is legitimate on integers but
not as an everywhere-defined real-exponent function; a>0,a!=1 belongs
to the usual real exponential convention, not the unrestricted sequence claim.
N's inclusion of zero and geometric-sequence nonzero-term requirements are
absent from seed. A substantive conditional validity/counterexample question
can be keyed only with its integer domain and recurrence definition visible.
Both unrestricted written proofs and resolution of the source convention
remain unavailable; do not silently prove a restricted theorem as the original.

708. Task: four consecutive-integer brackets.
30<30.2<31; 0<.49<1; -1<-2/3<0; -11<-10.8<-10.
This is convention-independent and does not need the floor notation.

709. Task: three integer/fractional decompositions, conditional floor.
105=105+0; -7.8=-8+.2; -21 2/7=-22+5/7.
Negative mixed number means -(21+2/7).

710. Task: four floor/fractional sums.
54.1=54+.1; 12.63=12+.63; -.97=-1+.03; -20.15=-21+.85.
Every fractional remainder is in [0,1), including negative inputs.

711. Task: six floors of positive/negative irrational roots.
sqrt(2) floors 1 and -sqrt(2) floors -2;
sqrt(13) floors 3 and -sqrt(13) floors -4;
sqrt(47) floors 6 and -sqrt(47) floors -7.
Negating a noninteger does not negate its floor.

712. Task: four nested operations.
{[17.3]}={17}=0; [{-25.7}]=[.3]=0;
{[15]}={15}=0; [{23}]=[0]=0.
General facts: fractional part of any integer is zero, floor of a remainder
in [0,1) is zero. These are all-real statements, not merely four memorized zeros.

713. Task: generate several examples for each of three inequalities.
a union over n in Z of [n,n+.15); e.g -1,-.9,0,.1,1.1.
b union (n+2/3,n+1); e.g -.2,.7,1.8 (2/3 itself excluded).
c let f={a}. For f<.95, {a+.05}=f+.05, so strict inequality fails.
For .95<=f<1, {a+.05}=f-.95, so the LHS exceeds RHS by 1.
Hence union [n+.95,n+1), e.g -.05,.96,1.99. Boundary .95 included.
Selecting valid witnesses/characterizations is not producing new examples.

714. Task: two full solution sets.
a 2<=2.75+x<3 =>[-.75,.25).
b equality of fractional parts iff inputs differ by an integer =>x in Z.
Unbounded integer lattice is not a single interval.

715. Task: six variable-specific solution sets.
a x in [-.21,.79).
b 2<=y-.36<3 =>y in [2.36,3.36).
c [-8.2]=-9, so -9<=1.7+z<-8 =>z in [-10.7,-9.7).
d x in Z.
e y-.39=n+.61 =>y=n+1 =>y in Z.
f 1.62+x=n+.65 =>x=n-.97, n in Z (equivalently Z+.03).
Equivalent lattice spellings must not be separate single-choice alternatives.

716. Task: produce a counterexample to each proposed equality.
a {5.41+x}={5.41}+{x} fails exactly when {x}>=.59;
e.g x=.6 gives .01 !=1.01. Integer shifts do not repair a carry.
b [2x]=2[x]+floor(2{x}); fails exactly when {x}>=.5;
e.g x=.5 gives 1 !=0. Equal endpoint .5 is important.

717. Task: decide product identity and justify.
It is not universal: a=b=1.5 gives {ab}=.25 but {a}{b}=.25,
so that pair is NOT a counterexample. Use a=1.2,b=2.3:
{ab}=.76, {a}{b}=.06; cross terms involving integer parts matter.
Also a=b=.8 yields .64=.64, a valid coincidence. A proper validity question
must distinguish genuine counterexamples from equality examples.

718. Task: decide additivity of floor and justify.
Let a=m+r,b=n+s, r,s in [0,1). floor(a+b)=m+n+floor(r+s).
Equality holds iff r+s<1. Example .6,.6 violates it (1 !=0);
negative examples are allowed, e.g -.2,-.3 give -1 !=-2.
The failure is a carry, not a universal defect for negative numbers.

719. Task: draw three all-real graphs.
a floor(x)+{x}=x, entire diagonal y=x.
b floor({x})=0 and c {floor(x)}=0, entire horizontal y=0.
No holes at integers. Recognition of actual visible graphs substitutes for
independent plotting; explicit convention still required.

720. Task: locate three plane point sets.
a [0,1) x R, left boundary included/right excluded.
b R x [1,2), lower included/upper excluded.
c [1,2) x [2,3), left/bottom included/right/top excluded; only lower-left
corner included among its four corners. Sets are filled, not boundary-only.

721. Task: plot floor(x)=floor(y).
Union over all n in Z of [n,n+1) x [n,n+1). Unit diagonal squares,
not just the line y=x and not the whole band |x-y|<1.
Left and bottom edges included; top and right excluded within each tile.
At every integer diagonal corner (n,n) the next tile includes that point.
Example (.2,.8) qualifies, (.9,1.1) does not despite separation <1.
A finite visible window must state the pattern repeats for all integer n.

722. Task: four expressions as base-10 powers.
a 10^(a+b); b 10^(3a); c 10^(b/2); d 10^(2a/3+b/5).
The original defines positive bases 31.5 and .42 so all real roots used
here are well-defined. Multiplication adds exponent contributions.

